phasizing the importance of understanding and addressing these factors.\nReference Content 2:\n**American Society of Regional Anesthesia and Pain Medicine (ASRA) Conducted Its First Conference on Local Anesthetic Toxicity in 2001** Following the release of levo-enantiomers of ropivacaine and l-bupivacaine, and the use of lipid emulsion as an antidote for LAST. This initial study highlighted the need for ongoing updates to practice advisories. In 2008, a second advisory panel was formed to develop a rational approach to treatment, incorporating information on the mechanisms, prevention, and detection of LAST. In 2015, the board of directors commissioned a group of researchers to develop the third advisory, emphasizing the importance of updating practice advisories with new knowledge and information. The focus is on preventing and detecting LAST, as well as improving case presentation and treatment protocols. The document underscores the need for a rational approach to treatment, with a particular emphasis on lipid resuscitation mechanisms and new insights into the epidemiology and patterns of case presentation. The updated practice advisories aim to reflect the latest findings and improve patient outcomes.\nstrongly for education and preparedness among all professionals who administer local anesthetics to their patients. The American Society of Regional Anesthesia and Pain Medicine (ASRA) convened its first Conference on Local Anesthetic Toxicity in 2001 coinciding with initial clinical experience after release of the levo-enantiomers ropivacaine and l-bupivacaine, plus emerging laboratory observations on the effectiveness of lipid emulsion as an antidote for LAST. In 2008, a second advi- sory panel met to develop a rational approach for treatment2 and to assimilate information on the history, mechanisms, prevention, and detection of LAST.1,3–7 Consistent with ASRA's commitment to update its practice advisories as new knowledge emerges, in 2015, the board of director